From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 17:17:32 +0200
Subject: dt-bindings: phy: add Intel GRX500 PCIe slim PHY

The serdes PHY behind each of the three root complexes, undocumented so
far. Its shape follows the USB PHY of the same family: one register
window, one reset, and a word in the chip-top syscon reached through
intel,syscon plus a byte offset.

Two things differ from the USB side and are the reason this needs saying
at all. All three instances share one chip-top word instead of having one
each, so the offset is the same everywhere and which instance is meant is
a bit position inside it, which is what intel,phy-id supplies. And the
LCPLL that intel,cgu-syscon points at is shared too, so intel,ssc-enable
is a request rather than a switch: the first instance to ask turns spread
spectrum on for all of them.

intel,phy-id stays a property of its own. It could plausibly ride along as
a second syscon argument, since the offset never varies and the id only
picks a bit inside that one word, but that is a different question with a
different answer and this file does not settle it -- it describes what the
driver reads today.

intel,ssc-enable is documented as the explicit 0 or 1 the boards spell.
The driver also accepts it as a bare flag; only the valued form is
sanctioned here, because that is the one in use and a property with two
spellings is worth having only one of.

reg-names is described but not required: there is a single window and the
driver maps it by index.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/intel,grx500-pciephy.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/intel,grx500-pciephy.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..4ea34576f0bc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/intel,grx500-pciephy.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,121 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/phy/intel,grx500-pciephy.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Intel GRX500 PCIe slim PHY
+
+maintainers:
+  - Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+
+description: |
+  The serdes PHY feeding one of the three PCIe root complexes of the Intel
+  MIPS xRX500 (GRX500) SoC family. Each root complex has a PHY of its own, and
+  the three instances differ only in their register window, their bit in the
+  first RCU reset bank, and which of them they are.
+
+  Two register blocks outside the PHY's own window take part. One is the
+  chip-top interface mux word, which holds this instance's reference-clock
+  gate; unlike the USB PHY's per-controller word, all three instances share
+  the same word and are told apart by a bit position inside it. The other is
+  the LCPLL and its spread-spectrum generator, which sit in the clock
+  generation unit and are shared by every instance, so the programming
+  sequence runs once no matter how many PHYs are enabled.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: intel,grx500-pciephy
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  reg-names:
+    items:
+      - const: phy
+
+  intel,syscon:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/phandle-array
+    items:
+      - items:
+          - description: phandle to the chip-top syscon
+          - description: >
+              byte offset within it of the interface mux word holding the
+              reference-clock gates of all three PHYs
+    description:
+      The reference-clock gate sits in the chip-top syscon rather than in the
+      PHY's own register window. The offset is the same for all three
+      instances; intel,phy-id selects the bit.
+
+  intel,cgu-syscon:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/phandle
+    description:
+      Phandle to the clock generation unit, which holds the LCPLL and the
+      spread-spectrum generator shared by all three PHYs.
+
+  intel,phy-id:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+    description:
+      Which of the three instances this is. It selects the instance's bit in
+      the shared interface mux word, and the driver cannot derive it from
+      anything else the node carries.
+    maximum: 2
+
+  intel,ssc-enable:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint32
+    description:
+      Whether spread-spectrum clocking is enabled on the shared LCPLL. It is
+      shared, so the first instance to ask for it turns it on for all of them.
+    enum: [0, 1]
+    default: 0
+
+  resets:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  reset-names:
+    items:
+      - const: phy
+
+  "#phy-cells":
+    const: 0
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+  - intel,syscon
+  - intel,cgu-syscon
+  - intel,phy-id
+  - resets
+  - reset-names
+  - "#phy-cells"
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    phy@18500000 {
+        compatible = "intel,grx500-pciephy";
+        reg = <0x18500000 0x100000>;
+        reg-names = "phy";
+        #phy-cells = <0>;
+        intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
+        intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
+        intel,phy-id = <0>;
+        intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+        resets = <&rcu_reset0 12 12>;
+        reset-names = "phy";
+    };
+
+  - |
+    phy@18a00000 {
+        compatible = "intel,grx500-pciephy";
+        reg = <0x18a00000 0x100000>;
+        reg-names = "phy";
+        #phy-cells = <0>;
+        intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
+        intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
+        intel,phy-id = <2>;
+        intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+        resets = <&rcu_reset0 10 10>;
+        reset-names = "phy";
+    };
